Z||000000||DaRkLoRD||03-13-2000||12:11 AM||||I'm going to buy a Fiero repair manual, but I'm not sure if I should get Haynes or Chiltons. Any major differences? Which one do you recommend?||||reg||1
Z||000001||BAM-BAM||03-13-2000||12:23 AM||||   Mylord, I have a chiltons for my Fiero, it's fairly good but some minor, yet important thing are left out. I also have a Haynes for my Grand Prix, Same thing. They seem to approach things in a "typical" format. Not being too specific on any one thing. Hope that helps you <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>BAM-BAM <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000002||Oreif||03-13-2000||12:51 AM||||For a really good manual watch Ebay and Yahoo auctions for the Factory service manuals. You can get it for your year and they have everything in them! Or you could order them from the Fiero store at $75.00. I've seen them auction off anywhere from $30 to $50. I have the Factory service manual and the Haynes. I ususally end up using the factory one.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||03-13-2000||12:54 AM||||Haynes & Chiltons are between $15-$20 tho.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||03-13-2000||01:36 AM||||For 1/4 the price you get 1/4 the detail.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaRkLoRD||03-13-2000||01:39 AM||||I don't even have a Fiero yet, I just want something where I can look up stuff.. I can get into more detail (factory manual) when I buy my GT, but until then.. a $20 manual is a little more to my budget's liking.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>so.. of those 2.. should I get a Haynes or a Chiltons?||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||03-13-2000||02:36 AM||||I have the Haynes and a Factory Manual. I don't have a Chiltons for the Fiero but have had them for other cars over the years. In general I think the Haynes are written a little better than the Chiltons. ||||reg||3
Z||000007||Phil||03-13-2000||06:26 AM||||Libraries usually have Chiltons. You can look at it for free.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||03-13-2000||08:51 AM||||I vote for Haynes.  There are plenty of procedures that aren't covered, but for a shadetree mechanic like me, it's pretty good.  Haynes covers common jobs like brakes, bearings, suspension, emissions/sensors, etc. in pretty good detail.<P>My mother bought me the "new" Chilton's for Christmas.  It's better than the "old" Chilton's manuals (which were best used a place to set your beer) but I still just use it as a supplement to Haynes.<P>Now if only I could track down a factory service manual...||||reg||1
